The attached image shows histological sections of mouse muscle two weeks after intramuscular injection with either aluminum hydroxide adjuvant (AH) (left panel) or Nano-11 (right panel), stained with H&E. The AH-injected site exhibits extensive granulomatous inflammation (asterisks) with dense aggregates of inflammatory cells and macrophages around skeletal muscle (M). In contrast, the Nano-11-injected site shows minimal residual inflammation (asterisk), with relatively few macrophages forming slender cords within the connective tissue, highlighting the transient and less severe inflammatory response induced by Nano-11 compared to AH. This supports Nano-11's potential as a safe vaccine adjuvant.